1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`

The function reads six loosely related properties from a generic `options` parameter. Although `CheckoutOptions` is named, its broad string fields allow an agent to swap values, add unrelated options, or overlook the serialization order without TypeScript objecting.

Refactor to function-owned, domain-grouped inputs and destructure them at the boundary:

```ts
interface CheckoutDescription {
  subscription: {
    plan: string;
    role: string;
  };
  locale: {
    region: string;
    currency: string;
  };
  payment: {
    status: string;
    shouldRetry: boolean;
  };
}

export function describeCheckout({
  subscription: { plan, role },
  locale: { region, currency },
  payment: { status, shouldRetry },
}: CheckoutDescription): string {
  return [
    plan,
    role,
    region,
    currency,
    status,
    String(shouldRetry),
  ].join(":");
}
```

This ties each new field to a clear concern and makes the formatter’s dependencies visible in its signature.

2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`

It currently returns three unrelated shapes with no explicit return type and 0% key overlap between some variants. An agent might access `ok` on a redirect, assume `url` exists on success, or add a fourth shape that callers fail to handle.

Use an explicit discriminated union:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | {
      kind: "error";
      code: string | undefined;
      message: string | undefined;
    }
  | {
      kind: "redirect";
      url: string | undefined;
      permanent: false;
    }
  | {
      kind: "success";
      value: string | undefined;
      cached: false;
    };

export function resolvePaymentResult(
  input: {
    error?: boolean;
    redirect?: boolean;
    code?: string;
    message?: string;
    url?: string;
    value?: string;
  },
): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return { kind: "error", code: input.code, message: input.message };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return { kind: "redirect", url: input.url, permanent: false };
  }

  return { kind: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
}
```

Callers must now narrow on `kind`, and TypeScript will flag unhandled variants. A further improvement would make the input discriminated too, eliminating the ambiguous `error && redirect` state.

3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`

The condition `!disableBilling && !skipRetry` requires two mental inversions. An agent adding another restriction could invert only one flag or accidentally change `&&` to `||`.

Expose positive state and return the predicate directly:

```ts
export function canRunBilling(
  isBillingEnabled: boolean,
  isRetryAllowed: boolean,
): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryAllowed;
}
```

Existing callers must pass the inverted old values:

```ts
canRunBilling(!disableBilling, !skipRetry);
```

Preferably, rename the state at its source so those negations disappear entirely. This also resolves the overlapping `boolean_naming_drift` finding for `disableBilling` and `skipRetry`.
